One package of each kind a coverage profile can contain, so that what discovery says about a
package can be checked against what the profile says about it.

panics is the case that matters. Its test dies, so go writes no data for the package at all — not
a zero, an absence — and the total is then computed over a smaller denominator. Measured on this
tree: reported 33.3% whether the lost package would have been 100.0% (true total 42.9%) or 0.0%
(true total 20.0%). The same number for opposite truths, so the error has no sign to correct for.

Nothing downstream can notice. A profile does not record what was meant to be in it, and
delegator's `|| true`, which exists so one broken package does not fail the build, is exactly the
line that turns this into a green run.

HasTests is the other half: it comes from the source, not the profile, so it still says panics was
meant to have been measured.
-- go.mod --
module states.test

go 1.27
-- covered/c.go --
package covered

func A() int { return 1 }
func B() int { return 2 }
-- covered/c_test.go --
package covered

import "testing"

func TestAB(t *testing.T) {
	if A()+B() != 3 {
		t.Fatal("no")
	}
}
-- partial/p.go --
package partial

func P() int { return 1 }
func Q() int { return 2 }
-- partial/p_test.go --
package partial

import "testing"

// Touches nothing of its own package, which is a zero the profile does record.
func TestNothing(t *testing.T) {
	if 1 != 1 {
		t.Fatal("no")
	}
}
-- panics/x.go --
package panics

func X() int { return 1 }
func Y() int { return 2 }
func Z() int { return 3 }
func W() int { return 4 }
-- panics/x_test.go --
package panics

import "testing"

func TestBoom(t *testing.T) {
	panic("the data for this package is now gone")
}
-- helper/h.go --
package helper

func H() int { return 42 }
-- notest/n.go --
package notest

func N() int { return 7 }
